y met. Evidence detail: the monitor writes a `suite-compliance-<ts>.json` only on a successful run — the five files on disk are all `measured`, and a timed-out cycle leaves **no** detail file, so absence of a file for a cycle is itself the observable of the timeout. Criterion (b) is what would let an auditor distinguish "just over budget" from "runaway"; it is still missing. **Active outage 2026-11-09T17:39Z:** `unreadable` has now repeated on **three consecutive cycles** (`1794245284`, `1794245583`, `1794245878`), and the newest successful detail file is still `suite-compliance-1794245019.json` (17:23Z) — so the lens has produced **no compliance reading for ~16 minutes**. "Intermittent" now understates it: while this persists, *nobody* can determine green-suite compliance, and that absence must not be read as either green or red. This remains a measurement-availability failure for the Executive/Judiciary to fix, not a legislative matter. **Outage closed 2026-11-09T18:02Z, with the cause still unexplained:** six consecutive `unreadable` cycles ran 17:28:04Z–17:52:45Z (≈29 min), then the lens returned **two consecutive** `measured` readings (`1794247045` 17:57:25Z and `1794247307` 18:01:47Z; both 1148 changes / 10 compliant / 1138 unreadable / 0 violations). So (a) is satisfied for this window and no compliance reading was lost silently — an unreadable cycle is visibly absent, not zero. **Still open:** (b), the missing per-reading wall time, which is exactly what would now show whether the run sits just above or far above 60 s; the underlying overshoot was never identified and nothing was repaired (`judiciary-measurements.py:105` unchanged). Treat this as a **recurring measurement risk, not a fixed defect** — the same overshoot can recur without warning, and the next `unreadable` cycle reopens the observation. |
